  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Cam Schlittler held them to two runs through six innings. The problem was the Yankees could only muster one.",
    "what_this_means": "They traded chances for six innings and found nothing. The game sat 1-1 until Toronto broke through in the seventh with two runs. The moment the window closed. This is what happens when pitching delivers and the offense refuses: a one-run loss that could have been prevented by a single hit in any of the six tied innings. Schlittler did his job. The lineup did not. They left a runner in scoring position in the second. They left two more in the seventh with the game still within reach. That inning alone was the difference between a split and a loss. One clean swing changes the math. They did not get it.",
    "what_to_watch": "Toronto ranks among baseball's weakest offenses. Carlos Rod\u00f3n has been borderline unhittable all season, carrying a 1.50 ERA. Tonight is a chance to punish that weakness and move back within striking distance.",
